    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always seen math as a tool, not a rigid set of rules. As a part-time student and someone who loves art and computer science, I find beauty in patterns and logic. I don’t fear math, but I appreciate it when it connects to real life—like in cooking or music. It’s not about perfection, but understanding and creativity.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Occasionally, yes—especially when the math feels abstract or disconnected from daily life. But I’ve learned to manage that anxiety through mindfulness and breaking problems into small steps. I remind myself that mistakes are part of learning, and I’m not alone in feeling this way. Yoga and classical music help me stay calm.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used an AI tutor app for algebra and calculus. It explained concepts step by step and gave me practice problems with instant feedback. It helped me visualize equations and made abstract ideas more concrete. I found it especially useful for checking my work and understanding errors. It didn’t replace my learning, but it supported it well.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b² - 4ac)] / (2a). Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac) to see if there are real or complex roots. If positive, you get two real solutions; if zero, one solution; if negative, complex ones. Practice with simple examples to build confidence.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "First, take the derivative f'(x) of the function. Set f'(x) = 0 and solve for x. These x-values are the critical points. Plug them back into the original function to find y-values. Then, use the second derivative test: if f''(x) > 0, it’s a local minimum; if f''(x) < 0, a local maximum. This helps identify where the function changes direction.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA reduces data complexity by finding directions (principal components) that capture most variance. First, standardize your data. Then, compute the covariance matrix and find its eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Sort by eigenvalues and pick top components. Project data onto these new axes. The math is complex, but it’s about patterns—like in art or music—so don’t panic. It’s a tool, not a barrier.",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs can personalize learning by adapting to each student’s pace and style. They can explain concepts in relatable ways—like through stories or analogies—linking math to real-life situations. They can offer instant feedback, suggest practice problems, and support students who feel anxious. With care, they can make math more inclusive and joyful, especially for diverse learners like myself."
